/* =============================================================================
   UTTARAKHAND — data for state-tool-engine.js. See assets/js/data/kerala.js for
   the schema reference, and assets/js/data/uttarpradesh.js / himachalpradesh.js
   for the two most directly relevant prior examples of this file's research/
   honesty discipline (primary-source extraction, rule-by-rule citation, honest
   hedging over invented figures, and independently resolving each state's
   current NPS/OPS status rather than assuming a blanket "restoration").

   THE PROVENANCE QUESTION THIS FILE WAS SPECIFICALLY ASKED TO RESOLVE — LIKE UP
   (ITS PARENT STATE), UTTARAKHAND NEVER WROTE A STANDALONE, RENUMBERED "CIVIL
   SERVICES (LEAVE) RULES" — IT STILL RUNS ON THE INHERITED UP FUNDAMENTAL/
   SUBSIDIARY RULES CODE, BUT HAS SELECTIVELY AMENDED PARTS OF IT WITH ITS OWN
   G.O.S/OFFICE MEMORANDA SINCE 2000: this is confirmed two ways this session,
   one of them a genuinely primary read. First, PRIMARY: the definitions
   section of "The Uttarakhand Retirement Benefits Act, 2018" (Act No. 17 of
   2018; full text read directly this session via pdftotext — clean, no OCR
   needed, from a prsindia.org-hosted PDF) itself defines "Emoluments" as "such
   salary which is defined under financial hand book volume-II part II to IV"
   — i.e. Uttarakhand's OWN 2018 Act points straight back at the same "UP
   Financial Handbook, Volume II, Parts II-IV" that uttarpradesh.js's own
   session read directly at budget.up.nic.in/Fin_H_Book/volume2/. Second,
   SECONDARY/SEMI-PRIMARY: an official Government of Uttarakhand training
   resource — a "Various Rules regarding leave and other service conditions"
   presentation by the Additional Director of the Uttarakhand Judicial and
   Legal Academy (ujala.uk.gov.in, a .uk.gov.in-hosted PDF, last updated
   11.7.2025, downloaded and pdftotext'd cleanly this session, no OCR needed)
   — cites leave provisions throughout by the SAME Fundamental Rule (FR) /
   Subsidiary Rule (SR) numbering uttarpradesh.js's session found in the UP
   Financial Handbook (FR 81-B, FR 83/83-A, FR 85, SR 201/202, etc.), confirming
   the base code is the same inherited text, not a Uttarakhand-renumbered one.
   IMPORTANT CAVEAT: this Academy presentation is itself training material
   (a LibreOffice Impress slide deck) that CITES rule/G.O. numbers — it is not
   the verbatim gazette/rule text itself, the way uttarpradesh.js's session
   read clean HTML FR/SR text directly. It is treated throughout this file as
   strong, officially-hosted corroboration of rule numbers and G.O. citations,
   not as an independently-verified primary gazette read — flagged per-type
   below where it is the sole source for a figure.

   HOW UTTARAKHAND HAS DIVERGED FROM UP SINCE BIFURCATION (9.11.2000) — A
   DIFFERENT PATTERN FROM UP'S OWN NEAR-TOTAL NON-DIVERGENCE: unlike UP (whose
   own file found no evidence of Paternity Leave, and Maternity Leave still
   frozen at UP's stale 1990-dated 3-month/90-day figure), Uttarakhand has
   affirmatively modernised several family-leave provisions via its own
   G.O.s/Office Memoranda since 2000 — Maternity Leave raised to 180 days
   (Office Memorandum No. 50/XXVII(7)/2009 dated 24.8.2009), a wholly new
   Paternity Leave created (G.O. No. 819/xxvii(7)34/2010-11 dated 31.12.2013 —
   a leave type that simply does not exist in UP's own current text), and a
   Child Care Leave scheme repeatedly amended since 2011 (see below). Separately,
   Uttarakhand also wrote its own wholly independent pension/gratuity/family-
   pension statute (the 2018 Act, above) and its own General Provident Fund
   rules (2006, below) rather than continuing to use UP's own CSR/GPF
   instruments — so the net picture is: INHERITED base leave code (FR/SR,
   unchanged in numbering), PLUS Uttarakhand-specific amendment-by-G.O. for
   several family-leave provisions, PLUS wholly independent, freshly-legislated
   pension/gratuity/family-pension and GPF instruments. This is a fourth
   distinct provenance pattern in this app's North-zone set (Punjab: still on
   PCSR verbatim; Haryana: rewrote PCSR into its own numbered code; HP: adopted
   the CENTRE's CCS Rules by reference; Uttarakhand: kept UP's FR/SR by
   reference but amended pieces of it directly, while writing brand-new
   pension/GPF law).

   METHODOLOGY / SEARCH-BUDGET NOTE: exactly as uttarpradesh.js's and
   himachalpradesh.js's own sessions documented, this session's WebSearch
   budget was reported exhausted (0 of 200 remaining) before the first query
   for this file could run — so every finding below that isn't a direct
   pdftotext read of a downloaded PDF came from WebFetch against
   html.duckduckgo.com search-result pages and the specific pages/PDFs
   surfaced that way (ujala.uk.gov.in, prsindia.org, indianemployees.com,
   gkmnews.in, namaskarlive.com, pioneeredge.in, drishtiias.com, and others,
   each named individually below). Two PDFs were downloaded and read via
   `pdftotext -layout` directly (both clean text layers, no legacy-Hindi-font
   garbling and no OCR needed — a genuinely better extraction situation than
   several other states' sessions in this app hit): the UJALA leave-rules
   presentation, and the Uttarakhand Retirement Benefits Act, 2018 itself.

   PENSION / NPS / OPS / UPS STATUS — THE OTHER FINDING THIS SESSION WAS
   SPECIFICALLY ASKED TO RESOLVE, AND A GENUINELY DIFFERENT, THREE-SCHEME
   PICTURE FROM EVERY OTHER STATE IN THIS APP SO FAR: "The Uttarakhand
   Retirement Benefits Act, 2018" (Act No. 17 of 2018), read directly and in
   full this session, states plainly in section 1(2): the Act applies to
   personnel "substantively appointed before the date of 1st October, 2005";
   personnel appointed substantively ON OR AFTER 1 October 2005 are governed
   instead by the "new contributory pension plan" (NPS). This 1.10.2005 cutoff
   date is Uttarakhand's OWN date — distinct from UP's own 1.4.2005 NPS cutoff,
   Rajasthan's/Punjab's 1.1.2004, and Chhattisgarh's 1.11.2004 — and is read
   directly from Uttarakhand's own primary legislative text, not inferred by
   analogy. TWO FURTHER LAYERS found this session, both SECONDARY-SOURCED (news
   reporting, not primary G.O. text, though internally consistent and
   cross-corroborating across two independent pioneeredge.in articles): (1) a
   NARROW OPS-OPTION for a cohort of roughly 6,000 already-retired employees
   "whose job advertisements/notifications were issued before the October 1,
   2005 notification that introduced the new pension scheme" but who were
   formally appointed on/after that date — i.e. the same recruitment-
   advertisement-vs-appointment-date narrowing this app's Rajasthan/Punjab/HP/UP
   files each independently found for their own states, following a March 2023
   Union Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions notification,
   adopted for Uttarakhand by a subsequent State Cabinet decision; and (2) a
   Uttarakhand Cabinet approval of the "Unified Pension Scheme" (UPS), reported
   effective 1 April 2025, offered as an OPTIONAL, one-time-irrevocable choice
   to NPS-covered employees (not a blanket conversion) — reported terms:
   assured pension of 50% of average basic pay of the last 12 months in service
   for 25+ years' qualifying service, minimum pension Rs. 10,000/month after a
   minimum 10 years' service, family pension at 60% of the pensioner's pension,
   a lump-sum payment of 1/10th of monthly emoluments for every completed six
   months of service, and dearness relief linked to the Consumer Price Index —
   consistent with the national UPS design the Government of India notified in
   2024, but NOT independently read from a primary Uttarakhand notification
   this session (drishtiias.com and pioneeredge.in, both current-affairs/policy
   news sites, are the sources). NET PICTURE: as of this session, an active
   Uttarakhand state employee may be on the OLD PENSION SCHEME under the 2018
   Act (appointed before 1.10.2005, or a member of the narrow ~6,000-person
   retiree-option cohort), on NPS (appointed on/after 1.10.2005 and did not opt
   into UPS), or on UPS (an NPS employee who has exercised the one-time 2025
   option) — do not assume any one of these without confirming your own
   appointment date and any option you may have exercised.

   THE 2018 ACT'S OWN FIGURES — READ DIRECTLY, HIGH CONFIDENCE: pension equals
   50% of last-drawn basic pay OR the average of the last 10 months' emoluments,
   whichever is more beneficial (s.7), subject to minimum/maximum pension
   amounts the Act itself says are "prescribed by the State Government" —
   i.e. the Act does NOT itself state a rupee minimum/maximum, and none is
   asserted below. No pension for under 10 years' qualifying service; full
   pension at 20 years; pro-rated between 10 and 20 years (s.7(a)-(c)).
   Retirement gratuity: half a month's emoluments per completed half-year of
   qualifying service, capped at 33 times last-drawn monthly emoluments or a
   lower ceiling the State Government may separately prescribe (Part 3(1) — no
   rupee ceiling given in the Act itself). Death gratuity (Part 3(2)): under 1
   year's service, 2x monthly emoluments; 1-5 years, 6x; 5-11 years, 12x; 11-20
   years, 20x; 20+ years, the same half-emoluments-per-half-year formula capped
   at 33x (or a lower State-prescribed ceiling). Family pension (Part IV, s.8):
   ordinarily 30% of last-drawn monthly salary, subject to a State-prescribed
   minimum/maximum (again no rupee figure given in the Act itself); an enhanced
   rate is payable for 10 years from the date of death, or until the deceased
   would have turned 67, WHICHEVER IS EARLIER — a genuinely Uttarakhand-specific
   figure this session flags explicitly, because it differs from the "7 years
   or age 65" pattern this app's other North-zone files (and the Centre's own
   CCS Pension Rules) use; the Act does not itself state the enhanced
   percentage, and none is asserted here beyond "increased rate." Eligible
   family members (s.9): spouse; sons under 25 (until self-supporting or age
   25, whichever earlier); unmarried/widowed/divorced daughters under 25 (with
   a specified order-of-priority mechanism for daughters beyond 25 where no
   other eligible family member exists); disabled/mentally challenged children
   of any age; parents, if no spouse/children survive. Commutation: up to 40%
   of pension, restorable 15 years after the date three months after PPO
   issue/first payment, whichever is earlier (s.10). Additional pension by age
   (s.11): 80-85 years, +20%; 85-90, +30%; 90-95, +40%; 95-100, +50%; 100+,
   +100% of basic pension/family pension — this table matches the same age-band
   structure the Centre's own CCS (Pension) Rules use, so it reads as plausible
   rather than surprising, but is asserted here because the Uttarakhand Act's
   own text states it directly. No pension for non-full-time/contract/work-
   charged/part-time/daily-wage/adhoc/fixed-salary service, unsanctioned leave
   periods, or other listed exclusions (s.1(2)(a)-(g)); no pension/family
   pension to a dismissed employee (s.13(2)).

   GENERAL PROVIDENT FUND — A GENUINELY UTTARAKHAND-SPECIFIC INSTRUMENT, NOT
   UP'S OWN 1985 GPF RULES: the "Uttaranchal General Provident Fund Rules,
   2006" (Notification No. 186, dated 8.3.2006 — confirmed via an
   indianemployees.com index entry; note the pre-rename name "Uttaranchal,"
   the state's own original 2000 name until its 1.1.2007 renaming to
   Uttarakhand, correctly preserved in this 2006 rule's own title) is
   Uttarakhand's OWN, separately-legislated GPF instrument — distinct from
   UP's own "General Provident Fund (Uttar Pradesh) Rules, 1985." This session
   found, via a secondary Hindi-language news-aggregator (gkmnews.in — NOT a
   primary notification text), that Rule 8(1) and Rule 8(4) of these Rules
   were substituted by the "Uttarakhand General Provident Fund (Amendment)
   Rules, 2025," effective 17.1.2025: Rule 8(1) now caps total annual GPF
   contribution at Rs. 5,00,000 per financial year (previously subject to a
   "cannot exceed one-third [of pay]"-type limit with no overall annual cap,
   per the same source's paraphrase); Rule 8(4) permits contribution
   DEcreases once a year and INcreases twice a year. This applies, by the
   logic of the 2018 Act's own 1.10.2005 cutoff, to the same pre-1.10.2005
   pension-eligible cohort — this session found no independent confirmation of
   that linkage in a primary GPF-rules eligibility clause (contrast HP's own
   file, which found and read HP's GPF Rule 4 eligibility clause directly).
   No current advance/withdrawal purpose list or ceiling figure was located
   this session — none is asserted below.

   GROUP INSURANCE — CONFIRMED BY NAME AND BY A SPECIFIC, DATED PREMIUM/COVER
   REVISION, BOTH SECONDARY-SOURCED: the "Uttarakhand State Employees Group
   Insurance and Savings Scheme" (उत्तराखंड राज्य कर्मचारी सामूहिक बीमा एवं
   बचत योजना) is confirmed to exist by name via an indianemployees.com circular
   index entry (Circular No. 37, dated 13.2.2009) — the circular's own
   substantive text was not read this session. A Hindi-language state-news
   source (namaskarlive.com, reporting a 22.12.2023 Cabinet decision formalised
   by a Finance Department order dated 12.1.2024, issued by the Additional
   Chief Secretary, Finance) gives a specific, dated revision, by PAY-BAND
   (bands that read as legacy/pre-revision pay-scale classification bands, used
   only to classify which premium/cover tier an employee falls into, not
   current gross pay — the same classification-band pattern UP's own 2005
   circular used, per uttarpradesh.js's file): Grade 1 (pay up to Rs. 2,800):
   premium Rs. 100 to Rs. 350/month, cover Rs. 1 lakh to Rs. 5 lakh; Grade 2
   (Rs. 2,801-5,400): premium Rs. 200 to Rs. 700/month, cover Rs. 2 lakh to
   Rs. 10 lakh; Grade 3 (above Rs. 5,401): premium Rs. 700 to Rs. 1,400/month,
   cover Rs. 2 lakh to Rs. 20 lakh; the insurance-fund/savings-fund split is
   reported as 50-50. This is a single secondary source, not independently
   corroborated against a primary Finance Department order this session —
   presented with that caveat; confirm your own current Group/band and
   cover/subscription amount with your DDO before relying on it.

   LEAVE — FIGURES AND THEIR SOURCES, TYPE BY TYPE, INCLUDING TWO FLAGGED
   CONFLICTS: Earned Leave (FR 81-B(1)) — the UJALA presentation and a
   separate, independent secondary source (himwantlive.com, a Uttarakhand-
   government-orders blog) BOTH confirm a maximum single-spell grant of 120
   days within India / 180 days if any portion is spent abroad (matching UP's
   own FR 81-B(1)(ii) figure) — but the two sources DISAGREE on the underlying
   ACCRUAL mechanism: himwantlive states a fixed half-yearly credit (16 days
   on 1 January, 15 days on 1 July = 31/year) with a 300-day accumulation
   ceiling, which reads as the more modern, Central-Government-pattern
   mechanism; UP's own file (and the general FR 81-B(1) text) instead describes
   a fractional one-eleventh-of-duty accrual with accrual ceasing once the
   balance reaches 180 days. This session could NOT independently resolve which
   accrual mechanism currently governs Uttarakhand — flagged explicitly in the
   type's hint rather than silently picking one, per this app's standing rule
   on conflicting figures. Medical Certificate Leave (FR 81-B(2)) — the UJALA
   presentation states a flat "365 days in entire service," while UP's own FR
   81-B(2) text (read directly by uttarpradesh.js's session) allows 12 months
   PLUS a further 6 months in exceptional Medical-Board-recommended cases (18
   months total) — also flagged as an unresolved conflict rather than
   resolved by assumption. Private Affairs Leave and Commuted Leave (FR
   81-B(3)/(4)) are modelled the same as UP's own file (Uttarakhand's own
   primary/semi-primary sources this session did not independently address
   these two), including UP's own well-documented structural quirk that
   Commuted Leave is tied to Study Leave (FR 84) specifically, not general
   medical commutation. Maternity Leave (180 days, per Office Memorandum No.
   50/XXVII(7)/2009 dated 24.8.2009), Miscarriage/Abortion Leave (42 days per
   occurrence, CMS of a Government Female Hospital's recommendation required —
   a more specific certifying-authority requirement than UP's own generic
   "Authorised Medical Attendant" language), and Paternity Leave (G.O. No.
   819/xxvii(7)34/2010-11 dated 31.12.2013, 15 days) are all read from the
   UJALA presentation directly. Child Care Leave is confirmed to exist (730
   days/2 years lifetime cap; minimum 15 days per spell; maximum 3 spells per
   calendar year) but its ORIGINATING G.O. is cited inconsistently across
   sources — himwantlive cites "G.O. No. 13/10/2002-A-1-2003 dated 4.1.2003,"
   the UJALA presentation cites "G.O. No. 207/xxvii(7)34/2011 dated
   13.10.2011," and the Department of School Education's own CCL-orders index
   (schooleducation.uk.gov.in) separately lists amendment orders dated
   30.5.2011, 10.4.2023, 1.6.2023 and 8.2.2024 — flagged explicitly as an
   unresolved multi-source citation conflict, consistent with this app's
   practice of flagging rather than silently picking one. Casual Leave (14
   days/year, maximum 10 at any one time absent exceptional circumstances) is
   corroborated by BOTH the UJALA presentation (citing SR 201/202 and G.O.
   Nos. 2093/II-B-181-1957 and 944-II-B-181/1957) and himwantlive
   independently — the highest-confidence leave figure in this file. Special
   Casual Leave (up to 4 days beyond the ordinary 14-day CL entitlement, for
   urgent/special reasons, per the same 1957/1962 G.O. sequence) and
   Compensatory Leave (for non-gazetted staff required to attend office on a
   holiday for extra work, per Manual of Government Orders para 1089 and G.O.
   No. 3/2/72-Apptt.(B) dated 26.2.1973) are both read from the UJALA
   presentation; Compensatory Leave is a type this session could confirm for
   Uttarakhand specifically that neither uttarpradesh.js nor himachalpradesh.js
   was able to confirm for its own state.

   DELIBERATELY OMITTED, WITH REASONS — Hospital Leave (SR 155/156): UP's own
   file already flags this UP provision as resting on an obviously-stale
   Rs. 300/month pay-eligibility ceiling and a narrow, dated, hazard-occupation
   list; this session found NO Uttarakhand-specific confirmation, amendment, or
   even mention of it (the UJALA presentation, which covers most other leave
   types in detail, does not address it) — omitted rather than repeating UP's
   admittedly-stale text under Uttarakhand's name with no added verification.
   Special Casual Leave on Transfer (SR 174(G), 6 days, convertible unused
   joining time within 6 months of transfer, per Finance (General) Section-1
   letters dated 17.9.1988 and 4.9.1989, per the UJALA presentation) is a real,
   well-cited but narrow provision — folded into the Special Casual Leave
   type's hint rather than given its own type, to avoid needless type
   proliferation for a niche transfer-specific scenario. House Building
   Advance is confirmed to exist (application-form documents were located, and
   an Accountant General (A&E) Uttarakhand office circular references "HBA
   Rules and Office Memorandum dated 9.11.2017" — but that circular reads as
   possibly specific to AG-office staff rather than a general state-wide
   scheme, and its full text was not read this session) — included below as a
   type, but with NO ceiling amount or interest rate asserted; confirm current
   terms with your DDO. Motor Vehicle Advance and Festival Advance: this
   session found NO Uttarakhand-specific evidence for either (unlike UP, where
   a structurally-different Diwali-bonus/pre-Holi-advance-salary mechanism was
   at least found under the "festival" heading) — both omitted entirely rather
   than assumed by analogy to other states. Study Leave (FR 84/SR 146-A,
   referenced only as the leave Commuted Leave is tied to) is omitted for the
   same reason every other state file in this app omits it: no prior state
   file models it as an applicant-facing type.
   ============================================================================= */
